---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../../components/Quiz.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="01-variables-types"
  lessonSlug="02-types-int-float"
  hook="Python's two numeric types — int for whole numbers, float for fractions — look similar but behave differently. Knowing when each one shows up will save you a lot of confusing bugs."
>
  <h2>Two kinds of numbers</h2>
  <p>
    In Python, <code>3</code> and <code>3.0</code> are not the same value. The
    first is an <strong>int</strong> (integer); the second is a <strong>float</strong>
    (floating-point number). You can ask Python which is which:
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    code={`print(type(3))
print(type(3.0))
print(type(3 + 4))
print(type(3 + 4.0))    # mixing int and float → result is float`}
  />

  <p>
    The rule for arithmetic: if all operands are <code>int</code>, the result is
    <code>int</code>. As soon as one <code>float</code> sneaks in, the whole
    expression "promotes" to <code>float</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Operators you'll use every week</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>+</code>&nbsp; addition</li>
    <li><code>-</code>&nbsp; subtraction</li>
    <li><code>*</code>&nbsp; multiplication</li>
    <li><code>/</code>&nbsp; <strong>true</strong> division — always returns a <code>float</code></li>
    <li><code>//</code> <strong>floor</strong> division — drops the remainder, returns the smaller type</li>
    <li><code>%</code>&nbsp; modulo — the remainder</li>
    <li><code>**</code> exponent — <code>2 ** 10</code> is 1024</li>
  </ul>

  <CodePlayground
    caption="The / vs // distinction trips up almost everyone"
    code={`print(10 / 4)     # → 2.5      (true division, always float)
print(10 // 4)    # → 2        (floor division, drops the .5)
print(10 % 4)     # → 2        (the remainder)

# // and % together = "how many full groups, and how many left over?"
minutes = 137
hours = minutes // 60
left  = minutes % 60
print(hours, "hours", left, "minutes")`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="/ always returns a float, even when it could be exact">
    <p>
      <code>10 / 5</code> evaluates to <code>2.0</code>, not <code>2</code>. If a
      downstream test asks for an <code>int</code>, you'll need <code>//</code> or
      <code>int(...)</code>. This catches a lot of students on their first quiz.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Converting between types</h2>
  <p>
    The functions <code>int()</code>, <code>float()</code>, and <code>str()</code>
    take a value and return a new value of the requested type. They do
    <strong>not</strong> change the original.
  </p>

  <CodePlayground
    code={`x = 5.9
print(int(x))        # → 5   (truncates toward zero, doesn't round!)
print(round(x))      # → 6   (this one rounds)

n = 7
print(float(n))      # → 7.0
print(str(n))        # → '7'  (now it's a string — useful for printing)

# What int() does to negatives surprises some people:
print(int(-5.9))     # → -5, not -6`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="int() truncates, round() rounds">
    <p>
      Reach for <code>int(x)</code> when you want to <em>drop</em> the fractional
      part. Reach for <code>round(x)</code> when you want the nearest whole
      number. They give different answers for anything not ending in <code>.5</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Quiz
    question="What does this print?  x = 7 / 2  → print(type(x))"
    choices={[
      { text: "<class 'int'>",   explain: "Nope — `/` is true division, which always returns a float." },
      { text: "<class 'float'>", correct: true, explain: "Right. Even though 7 and 2 are both ints, `/` promotes the result to float." },
      { text: "<class 'number'>", explain: "Python doesn't have a single 'number' type — it has int, float, complex, etc." },
      { text: "3.5",             explain: "That's the *value* x holds. The question asked for its *type*." },
    ]}
  />

  <Quiz
    question="`x = int(3.9)` — what is x?"
    choices={[
      { text: "4 — int() rounds", explain: "Close, but int() *truncates*, not rounds. Use round() if you want rounding." },
      { text: "3", correct: true, explain: "Right. int() drops everything after the decimal point: 3.9 becomes 3." },
      { text: "3.0", explain: "int(...) returns an int, not a float. 3.0 would be the float version." },
      { text: "Error — can't convert a float to int", explain: "This conversion is perfectly legal in Python." },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>The exercise in WolfCode</h2>
  <p>
    Open <code>02-types-int-float.py</code>. Four variables are set to
    <code>None</code> — replace each with the correct expression. The tests
    check both the <em>value</em> and the <em>type</em>.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="note" title="Hint scaffolding">
    <p>
      Read the comment above each blank — it tells you what the expected type is
      (int or float). If you produce the right number but the wrong type, the
      test will say so explicitly, e.g. "expected int, got float".
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
